  "applicability_scope": [
    "Level 1 review reported by Peng Zhang of two mathematical statements, each in public and canonical presentations with identical normalized types.",
    "Theorem 2.1: semantic hallucination detectability iff stable-index identification, on nonempty countable types with Nat-indexed families and exact positive presentations. Finite targets and duplicate languages are allowed; empty targets have no positive presentation.",
    "Theorem 2.3: universal semantic detection given complete, correctly labeled domain enumerations. Negative examples certify target nonmembership. Correctness on arbitrary types is conditional on such an enumeration, with unpresentable cases vacuous.",
    "Finite-history detectors use finite adaptive query trees for membership in an arbitrary fixed G; eventual correctness is G-subset-target. The stabilization threshold can depend on target, presentation, and candidate set.",
    "Recursive project-local definition bodies, finite query-tree constructors and evaluation machinery preserve statement meaning; fingerprinting them does not certify detector constructions or theorem proofs."
  ],
